---
import InternalLayout from '../../layouts/InternalLayout.astro';
---

<InternalLayout
  title="Retainer Structure"
  description="Internal reference for how Benefactor should structure retainers, pass-through media budgets, approvals, and scope controls."
  eyebrow="Commercial Policy"
  intro="This page captures the default Benefactor model for retainers: agency fees billed in advance, clear scope boundaries, and separate handling for pass-through spend such as ad budget, software, or third-party tools."
  backHref="internal/"
>
  <section class="internal-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="internal-section__header">
        <span class="internal-section__eyebrow">Default Model</span>
        <h2>How Benefactor should get paid on retainer</h2>
        <p>
          For recurring engagements, the cleanest setup is a monthly retainer billed in advance
          for Benefactor's labor, strategy, execution, reporting, and operating cadence. That
          retainer is separate from any spend that flows to ad platforms, tools, or third-party
          vendors.
        </p>
      </div>

      <table class="internal-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Budget Line</th>
            <th>What It Covers</th>
            <th>How It Should Work</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Agency Retainer</td>
            <td>Strategy, campaign management, landing pages, reporting, meetings, optimization, and internal execution.</td>
            <td>Billed monthly in advance, due before the service month begins.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Pass-Through Ad Spend</td>
            <td>Google Ads, Meta, LinkedIn, programmatic, or other direct media costs.</td>
            <td>Should remain separate from the retainer and tied to a client-owned or client-controlled funding method.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Third-Party Tools</td>
            <td>Creative tools, call tracking, landing page software, data enrichers, and platform subscriptions.</td>
            <td>Either billed directly to the client or approved as reimbursable with a documented ceiling.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="internal-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="internal-section__header">
        <span class="internal-section__eyebrow">Scope Control</span>
        <h2>What the retainer should explicitly define</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="internal-grid internal-grid--2">
        <article class="internal-card">
          <h3>Commercial guardrails</h3>
          <ul class="internal-list">
            <li>Monthly retainer amount and billing date</li>
            <li>Included channels, deliverables, and cadence</li>
            <li>Approval threshold for out-of-scope work</li>
            <li>Who owns ad accounts, data, creatives, and landing pages</li>
            <li>What happens if spend needs to increase mid-cycle</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="internal-card">
          <h3>Operating guardrails</h3>
          <ul class="internal-list">
            <li>Reporting cadence, usually weekly or biweekly with a monthly review</li>
            <li>Named approvers on the client side</li>
            <li>Expected response windows for launch approvals or creative signoff</li>
            <li>What counts as urgent work vs scheduled optimization</li>
            <li>What happens if approvals delay media execution</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="internal-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="internal-section__header">
        <span class="internal-section__eyebrow">Recommended Position</span>
        <h2>Preferred retainer stance for Benefactor</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="internal-note">
        <p>
          <strong>Preferred structure:</strong> Benefactor charges a monthly retainer for the
          work, while the client separately funds media spend and third-party platform costs.
          That keeps agency revenue clean, avoids muddling labor fees with pass-through spend,
          and gives both sides clearer reporting.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="internal-grid internal-grid--2" style="margin-top: var(--space-xl);">
        <article class="internal-card">
          <h3>Why this is cleaner</h3>
          <ul class="internal-list">
            <li>It simplifies accounting and tax treatment.</li>
            <li>It avoids the agency accidentally financing media with its own cash flow.</li>
            <li>It gives the client visibility into actual media and platform costs.</li>
            <li>It makes overages and approvals easier to govern.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="internal-card">
          <h3>What to avoid</h3>
          <ul class="internal-list">
            <li>Bundling large ad budgets into the retainer without separate controls.</li>
            <li>Letting reimbursable spend accumulate without a written ceiling.</li>
            <li>Fronting large media budgets unless there is a clear commercial reason.</li>
            <li>Using vague language about "miscellaneous marketing costs."</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</InternalLayout>
